# Prerequisites
Before creating an EKS cluster, ensure you have:
1. AWS Account
An active AWS account with permissions to create:
	EKS 
	EC2 
	IAM 
	VPC 
	CloudFormation 

2. Install AWS CLI
Amazon Linux

sudo yum install -y awscli-----aws --version

3. Configure AWS CLI

aws configure

aws sts get-caller-identity

4. Install kubectl
Download Latest Version

curl -LO "https://dl.k8s.io/release/$(curl -L -s \
https://dl.k8s.io/release/stable.txt)/bin/linux/amd64/kubectl"
Make Executable

chmod +x kubectl
Move to PATH

sudo mv kubectl /usr/local/bin/
Verify

kubectl version --client

5. Install eksctl
eksctl is the easiest way to create EKS clusters.
Amazon Linux

curl --silent --location \
"https://github.com/weaveworks/eksctl/releases/latest/download/eksctl_$(uname -s)_amd64.tar.gz" \
| tar xz -C /tmp

sudo mv /tmp/eksctl /usr/local/bin
Verify

eksctl version

Create EKS Cluster
Basic Cluster Creation

eksctl create cluster \
  --name my-cluster \
  --region us-west-2 \
  --nodegroup-name my-nodes \
  --node-type c7i-flex.large \
  --nodes 2 \
  --nodes-min 1 \
  --nodes-max 2 \
  --managed

Parameters
Parameter
Description
--name
Cluster Name
--region
AWS Region
--nodegroup-name
Node Group Name
--node-type
EC2 Instance Type
--nodes
Desired Nodes
--nodes-min
Minimum Nodes
--nodes-max
Maximum Nodes
--managed
Managed Node Group

What Happens Behind the Scenes?
EKS creates:
	VPC 
	Public/Private Subnets 
	Security Groups 
	EKS Control Plane 
	Managed Node Group 
	Auto Scaling Group 
	IAM Roles 

Verify Cluster Creation
List clusters:

eksctl get cluster---Update kubeconfig
Configure kubectl to access the cluster.

aws eks update-kubeconfig \
  --region us-west-2 \
  --name my-cluster      ----------Verify Kubernetes Access
Check nodes:
 kubectl get nodes

Check cluster info:

kubectl cluster-info

Useful kubectl Commands
View nodes:

kubectl get nodes
View pods:

kubectl get pods -A
View namespaces:

kubectl get ns
View services:

kubectl get svc -A

Deploy a Test Application
Create deployment:

kubectl create deployment nginx \
--image=nginx

Expose service:

kubectl expose deployment nginx \
--type=LoadBalancer \
--port=80
Check service:

kubectl get svc
AWS will provision an Elastic Load Balancer automatically.

Scale Deployment

kubectl scale deployment nginx \
--replicas=3
Verify:

kubectl get pods

Create Additional Node Group

eksctl create nodegroup \
  --cluster my-cluster \
  --name app-nodes \
  --node-type t3.medium \
  --nodes 2

Check:

kubectl get nodes

Delete EKS Cluster
When finished, delete all resources:

eksctl delete cluster \
--name my-cluster \
--region us-west-2

This removes:
	EKS Cluster 
	Node Groups 
	Load Balancers 
	Security Groups 
	CloudFormation Stacks 

Troubleshooting  No Nodes Found

kubectl get nodes
If empty:

aws eks update-kubeconfig \
--region us-west-2 \
--name my-cluster

Check Node Group

eksctl get nodegroup \
--cluster my-cluster

Check Cluster Status

aws eks describe-cluster \
--name my-cluster \
--query cluster.status

Check Worker Node EC2 Instances

aws ec2 describe-instances \
--filters "Name=tag:eks:cluster-name,Values=my-cluster"


Production Recommendations
	Use private subnets for worker nodes. 
	Enable cluster logging. 
	Use managed node groups. 
	Install AWS Load Balancer Controller. 
	Install Cluster Autoscaler. 
	Use IAM Roles for Service Accounts (IRSA). 
	Use EBS CSI Driver for persistent volumes. 
	Enable monitoring with CloudWatch and Prometheus. 
For learning and practice, start with a 2-node managed cluster and deploy NGINX, then explore Deployments, Services, Ingress, ConfigMaps, Secrets, and Horizontal Pod Autoscaling
